# What does NOT go into the build context.
#
# Beyond upload speed, two entries here are correctness rather than tidiness. A
# stray *.local env file would be copied by COPY . . and would then OVERRIDE
# env/.env.production, so the image would quietly be built against whatever a developer
# last pointed their laptop at — a localhost API inside a production image, with
# nothing failing anywhere. And node_modules from a Windows machine holds native
# binaries for the wrong platform, which npm ci in the build stage must be free to
# replace rather than inherit.

# Installed fresh inside the image, for linux/amd64.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output — produced by the build stage, never copied in.
dist
dist-ssr
*.tsbuildinfo

# ⚠ THE NATIVE TARGET IS THE BIGGEST WIN HERE. The Android project carries a
#   Gradle cache, a copy of the last synced web bundle and the merged-assets tree
#   — hundreds of megabytes that no web build reads, and which would also make
#   every COPY . . layer churn on each cap sync.
android
ios
.gradle

# ⚠ Developer env overrides only. env/.env.production is deliberately NOT excluded: it is
#   the configuration this image is built from.
env/*.local
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# History and tooling the build never reads.
.git
.github
.vscode
.idea
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage
.nyc_output

# Docs.
*.md
!README.md

# OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
